Since string are immutable in js, there are not mutator methods (all methods return new values without modifying
original string)

A. Accessor Methods (Extract information without modification)

1. charAt()
Signature: str.charAt(index)
Returns: String (or empty string "")
Description: Returns the character at the specified index.
Example:
"hello".charAt(1); // "e"
"hello".charAt(10); // ""

2. charCodeAt()
Signature: str.charCodeAt(index)
Returns: Number (UTF-16 code unit)
Description: Returns the UTF-16 code unit of the character at the index.
Example:
"hello".charCodeAt(1); // 101

3. codePointAt()
Signature: str.codePointAt(index)
Returns: Number (Unicode code point)
Description: Returns the full Unicode code point (supports emojis/surrogate pairs).
Example:
"😊".codePointAt(0); // 128522

4. includes()
Signature: str.includes(searchString[, position])
Returns: Boolean
Description: Checks if the string contains the specified substring.
Example:
"hello".includes("ell"); // true
"hello".includes("x"); // false

5. indexOf()
Signature: str.indexOf(searchString[, fromIndex])
Returns: Number (index or -1)
Description: Returns the first index of the specified substring.
Example:
"hello".indexOf("l"); // 2
"hello".indexOf("x"); // -1

6. lastIndexOf()
Signature: str.lastIndexOf(searchString[, fromIndex])
Returns: Number (index or -1)
Description: Returns the last index of the specified substring.
Example:
"hello".lastIndexOf("l"); // 3
"hello".lastIndexOf("x"); // -1

7. startsWith()
Signature: str.startsWith(searchString[, position])
Returns: Boolean
Description: Checks if the string starts with the specified substring.
Example:
"hello".startsWith("hel"); // true
"hello".startsWith("lo"); // false

8. endsWith()
Signature: str.endsWith(searchString[, length])
Returns: Boolean
Description: Checks if the string ends with the specified substring.
Example:
"hello".endsWith("llo"); // true
"hello".endsWith("hel"); // false

B. Transformation Methods (Return new modified strings)

1. toUpperCase()
Signature: str.toUpperCase()
Returns: String
Description: Converts the string to uppercase.
Example:
"hello".toUpperCase(); // "HELLO"

2. toLowerCase()
Signature: str.toLowerCase()
Returns: String
Description: Converts the string to lowercase.
Example:
"HELLO".toLowerCase(); // "hello"

3. trim()
Signature: str.trim()
Returns: String
Description: Removes whitespace from both ends of the string.
Example:
"  hello  ".trim(); // "hello"

4. trimStart() / trimLeft()
Signature: str.trimStart() or str.trimLeft()
Returns: String
Description: Removes whitespace from the start of the string.
Example:
"  hello".trimStart(); // "hello"

5. trimEnd() / trimRight()
Signature: str.trimEnd() or str.trimRight()
Returns: String
Description: Removes whitespace from the end of the string.
Example:
"hello  ".trimEnd(); // "hello"

6. padStart()
Signature: str.padStart(targetLength[, padString])
Returns: String
Description: Pads the start of the string until it reaches the target length.
Example:
"7".padStart(3, "0"); // "007"

7. padEnd()
Signature: str.padEnd(targetLength[, padString])
Returns: String
Description: Pads the end of the string until it reaches the target length.
Example:
"7".padEnd(3, "0"); // "700"

8. repeat()
Signature: str.repeat(count)
Returns: String
Description: Returns a new string repeated count times.
Example:
"ha".repeat(3); // "hahaha"

9. replace()
Signature: str.replace(searchValue, replaceValue)
Returns: String
Description: Replaces the first match of searchValue with replaceValue.
Example:
"hello".replace("l", "x"); // "hexlo"

10. replaceAll()
Signature: str.replaceAll(searchValue, replaceValue)
Returns: String
Description: Replaces all matches of searchValue with replaceValue.
Example:
"hello".replaceAll("l", "x"); // "hexxo"

C. Extraction Methods (Returns parts of the string)

1. slice()
Signature: str.slice(startIndex[, endIndex])
Returns: String
Description: Extracts a section of the string (supports negative indices).
Example:
"hello".slice(1, 3); // "el"
"hello".slice(-3); // "llo"

2. substring()
Signature: str.substring(startIndex[, endIndex])
Returns: String
Description: Similar to slice() but doesn't support negative indices.
Example:
"hello".substring(1, 3); // "el"
"hello".substring(2); // "llo"

3. substr() (Deprecated)
Signature: str.substr(startIndex[, length])
Returns: String
Description: Extracts length characters from startIndex (avoid, use slice()).
Example:
"hello".substr(1, 3); // "ell"

4. split()
Signature: str.split([separator[, limit]])
Returns: Array
Description: Splits the string into an array of substrings.
Example:
"a,b,c".split(","); // ["a", "b", "c"]
"hello".split(""); // ["h", "e", "l", "l", "o"]

D. String Combination and Conversion methods

1. concat()
Signature: str.concat(str2[, ...strN])
Returns: String
Description: Combines multiple strings.
Example:
"Hello".concat(" ", "World"); // "Hello World"

2. toString()
Signature: str.toString()
Returns: String
Description: Returns the string itself (used for object conversion).
Example:
const strObj = new String("hello");
strObj.toString(); // "hello"

3. valueOf()
Signature: str.valueOf()
Returns: String
Description: Returns the primitive value of the string.
Example:
const strObj = new String("hello");
strObj.valueOf(); // "hello"